      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "The unique Russian Eurasian civilization developed according to special patterns, starting from Ancient Rus, Muscovite Rus, the Moscow-Petersburg Empire, the Soviet civilization and up to post-Soviet Russia. A certain idea of the transformation of historical elites in the conditions of the early feudal monarchy, the estate-representative, absolutist monarchy, the communist system and post-Soviet development was formed. The political elite is considered by Marxists as the top part of the ruling class in each socio-economic system, including the USSR. In the works of V.I. Lenin there are characteristics of most elite groups, including the professional core of the new type of party that he created. After the revolution, the Bolshevik political elite gave a powerful impetus to the formation of a Soviet-type civilization - a stage in the development of the Russian Eurasian civilization. Led by the next generation of the communist elite, the Soviet system became the cumulative result of the activities of millions of Russian citizens. The intellectual stratum within the ruling party elite was constantly searching for new options for the country's development, which was manifested in the creation of platforms for various oppositions. The repressions of Stalinism directed the development of the elite and the system towards left-communist totalitarianism, which proved to be quite productive in the conditions of World War II. After the victorious Great Patriotic War, bureaucratization, overproduction and partial disintegration of the nomenclature elite intensified in the party. While the economy was developing steadily, even at a slow pace, there was no socio-political crisis, and the country's leadership refused to change the elites. The communist elite lost its leadership potential and authority among the people. During perestroika, a belated one-time renewal of the nomenclature elite began, which ended in the collapse of the party and the entire Soviet state. The newly created elitocracy has little in common with democracy and people's power; moreover, the concept of democratic elitism turned out to be a chimera. It has been revealed that the analysis of the process of elitogenesis in all its manifestations, especially in the Soviet period of development, is necessary to ensure the timely rotation of political elites and the implementation of social reforms.",
